WEBVTT

1
00:00:09.534 --> 00:00:18.387
In 793 a monk described a frightening attack on a monastery in England.

2
00:00:18.388 --> 00:00:22.390
Suddenly giant men jumped from the long ships.

3
00:00:22.391 --> 00:00:27.253
They were a head taller than any of us, and some had bright red hair.

4
00:00:27.254 --> 00:00:36.541
They had all manner of armor and weapons, and they killed every monk they saw, even though we had no weapons. Luckily I escaped.

5
00:00:36.542 --> 00:00:46.296
When I returned to the monastery, anything made of silver or gold was gone—everything of value. Who were these strange, terrifying warriors?

6
00:00:46.297 --> 00:00:48.831
The warriors were Vikings.

7
00:00:48.832 --> 00:00:56.267
From the eighth to the eleventh century, the Vikings raided the coasts of Europe during an era now called the Viking Age.

8
00:00:56.268 --> 00:01:01.286
England, France, Scotland, and Ireland were prey to their attacks.

9
00:01:01.287 --> 00:01:08.448
The Vikings came from the Scandinavian countries in northern Europe now known as Norway, Sweden, and Denmark.

10
00:01:08.449 --> 00:01:13.206
They were also called Norsemen, which means men from the north.

11
00:01:13.207 --> 00:01:20.973
Viking warriors stole whatever they found and killed many people, usually attacking with iron swords and large battle-axes.

12
00:01:20.974 --> 00:01:28.099
They wore skillfully made metal armor, and for defense, they carried round wooden shields with a center made of iron.

13
00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:35.841
Today many pictures show Vikings wearing helmets with horns, but this idea is an error that began in the nineteenth century.

14
00:01:35.842 --> 00:01:40.100
Most Viking warriors actually wore simple iron caps.

15
00:01:40.101 --> 00:01:42.983
At home the Vikings were farmers.

16
00:01:42.984 --> 00:01:51.300
They kept cattle, pigs, ducks, and chickens, and their farmlands produced crops like wheat and rye, as well as vegetables.

17
00:01:51.301 --> 00:01:55.710
Early spring was a busy time as they prepared their crops for planting.

18
00:01:55.711 --> 00:02:01.898
With help from slaves and servants, they plowed and fertilized the fields and planted seeds for grains.

19
00:02:01.899 --> 00:02:08.231
And then, after this spring work was finished, the Vikings left to attack foreign lands.

20
00:02:08.232 --> 00:02:14.968
The Vikings set sail in their boats to raid monasteries and villages for precious metals and for people to enslave.

21
00:02:14.969 --> 00:02:20.763
Carved from wood, their "longships" enabled the Vikings to travel swiftly and efficiently.

22
00:02:20.764 --> 00:02:25.346
The ships were light and long, with high curves at the front and back.

23
00:02:25.347 --> 00:02:29.476
They had oars along their entire length and a square sail above,

24
00:02:29.477 --> 00:02:34.719
so the Vikings could travel on the sea and then use the oars to make a speedy attack.

25
00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:40.105
Many of the ships also had a tall, fierce-looking dragon carved into their prows.

26
00:02:40.106 --> 00:02:44.902
When villagers saw one of these ships approaching, they often fled in terror!

27
00:02:44.903 --> 00:02:50.212
Because their boats were shallow, the Vikings were also able to travel inland by river.

28
00:02:50.213 --> 00:02:53.272
Their trade routes reached deep into Europe.

29
00:02:53.273 --> 00:02:59.249
France, Poland, and Russia were all part of the dynamic world of Viking commerce.

30
00:02:59.250 --> 00:03:07.355
Eventually the Vikings realized that many of the countries they raided (or traded with) were better for farming than their own homeland.

31
00:03:07.356 --> 00:03:14.939
They began to establish forts or settlements, and from these strongholds, they continued their raids and conquered new territory.

32
00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:23.529
The Vikings' ships were small, but they were very sturdy, which allowed the Vikings to travel to faraway places and become great explorers.

33
00:03:23.530 --> 00:03:26.402
Iceland was first settled by Vikings.

34
00:03:26.403 --> 00:03:33.201
When the famous Viking Erik the Red was banished from Iceland, he set sail in his longship in search of adventure.

35
00:03:33.202 --> 00:03:39.651
He discovered Greenland, and one of his sons, Leif Erikson, was the first European to reach North America.

36
00:03:39.652 --> 00:03:48.168
Leif sailed west of Greenland and discovered Baffin Island, Labrador, and Newfoundland almost five hundred years before Columbus reached America.

37
00:03:48.169 --> 00:03:55.237
Strangely enough, despite their warlike manner, the Vikings often adapted to, and joined the people they conquered.

38
00:03:55.238 --> 00:03:58.876
Over time the Vikings who conquered England became English;

39
00:03:58.877 --> 00:04:04.305
the Vikings who conquered Normandy quickly became part of its native French-speaking population.

40
00:04:04.306 --> 00:04:09.023
While the Vikings were raiders and pirates, they were also traders and merchants.

41
00:04:09.024 --> 00:04:13.873
Their trading ships carried fur, ivory, amber, and slaves.

42
00:04:13.874 --> 00:04:19.197
The Vikings were superb metalworkers, so they exported swords and other weapons.

43
00:04:19.198 --> 00:04:27.340
And some Vikings worked as mercenaries. They were never a unified group; instead they were separate bands of sea pirates.

44
00:04:27.341 --> 00:04:35.993
The Vikings are often portrayed as barbaric savages, but they were well organized and had a written language called Runic.

45
00:04:35.994 --> 00:04:43.157
The Vikings' mastery of this written language helped them conquer many opponents because it allowed them to send messages with ease.

46
00:04:43.158 --> 00:04:50.043
Much of their heritage survives today in the Norse sagas, which describe the adventures of their heroes and gods.

47
00:04:50.044 --> 00:04:56.874
These great legends inspired famous operas and poems, written many centuries after the Viking Age ended.

48
00:04:56.875 --> 00:05:00.032
The Viking heritage is still remembered today.

49
00:05:00.033 --> 00:05:03.460
In Scandinavia there are several Viking ship museums,

50
00:05:03.461 --> 00:05:09.671
where people can see restored Viking ships and admire the amazing craftsmanship of these bold sailors.

51
00:05:09.672 --> 00:05:12.968
And many cities hold annual Viking festivals.

52
00:05:12.969 --> 00:05:19.078
People can live and dress as Vikings, and craft weapons and jewelry using traditional Viking methods.

53
00:05:19.079 --> 00:05:22.975
Some people even participate in mock Viking battles!

54
00:05:22.976 --> 00:05:29.468
During the Viking age, the "men from the north" explored distant lands—and terrorized many people.

55
00:05:29.469 --> 00:05:38.721
With their longships and thirst for adventure, they left a permanent mark on both ship technology and European history.
